// ═══ PINNING EXAMPLE (Java 21) ═══

// (Avoid) PINS carrier — virtual thread can't unmount:
synchronized (sharedLock) {
    var result = httpClient.send(request, BodyHandlers.ofString());
    // Carrier thread BLOCKED during entire HTTP call!
    // Other virtual threads waiting for this carrier are STARVED.
}

// (Recommended) NO PINNING — ReentrantLock allows unmounting:
private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();

lock.lock();
try {
    var result = httpClient.send(request, BodyHandlers.ofString());
    // Virtual thread UNMOUNTS during HTTP wait → carrier serves others!
} finally {
    lock.unlock();
}

// ═══ DETECT PINNING ═══
// JVM flag:
// java -Djdk.tracePinnedThreads=short -jar app.jar
// Prints stack trace whenever pinning occurs

// JFR event (production-safe):
// jdk.VirtualThreadPinned event recorded automatically
// Analyze with: jfr print --events jdk.VirtualThreadPinned recording.jfr

// ═══ JAVA 24 (JEP 491): synchronized PINNING REMOVED ═══
// In Java 24, synchronized blocks NO LONGER pin virtual threads.
// The monitor implementation was rewritten to support unmounting.
// ReentrantLock is STILL preferred where you need:
//   - tryLock(timeout) — timed acquisition
//   - lockInterruptibly() — interruptible
//   - Multiple conditions (newCondition())
